  "description": "Learn your very first Portuguese words and sentences — people, food, and the verbs to be, to eat, and to drink.",
  "guidebook": "Welcome to Portuguese! 🇧🇷🇵🇹\n\n**Articles matter!** Every Portuguese noun has a gender. *O* = the (masculine), *a* = the (feminine). For example, *o homem* (the man) and *a mulher* (the woman).\n\n**Ser vs. Estar** — both mean \"to be.\" In this unit we use **ser** for identity: *Eu sou* (I am), *Você é* (You are).\n\n**Verbs ending in -ar:** *comer* (to eat) → *eu como*, *você come*. *Beber* (to drink) → *eu bebo*, *você bebe*.\n\n**Word order** is flexible, but Subject–Verb–Object is most common: *Eu como a maçã* (I eat the apple).\n\n**Tip:** Brazilians often drop the subject pronoun because the verb form already tells you who's acting. So *Como pão* = \"I eat bread.\"\n\nLet's go! 🚀",
